#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Move a user's hyprlang Hyprland config to the Lua module layout.

Hyprland 0.55 deprecated hyprlang and gave it "1-2 releases starting from
0.55". The image ships 0.56.2, so every ~/.config/hypr/hyprland.conf Rime has
ever seeded is one release from not loading. This converts one.

    ~/.config/hypr/hyprland.conf                 ~/.config/hypr/hyprland.lua
    ~/.config/hypr/apex-input.conf         ──►   ~/.config/hypr/rime/input.lua
    ~/.config/hypr/apex-display.conf             ~/.config/hypr/rime/monitors.lua
    ~/.config/rime-shell/ApexShellKeybinds.conf  ~/.config/hypr/rime/shell-keybinds.lua
                                                 ~/.config/hypr/rime/user-overrides.lua

The left column keeps its APEX names on purpose (rime-rename: keep): every
hyprlang config was seeded by an APEX image, and those are the files on disk.

## The rule that matters

Andre's own seeded hyprland.conf tells the reader "it is your file now, edits
here persist". So the config INVITED people to edit it, and a directive this
script does not understand must never be dropped on the floor. Anything not
recognised is written verbatim into a comment block in rime/user-overrides.lua
and listed in the migration report next to it. The worst outcome allowed here
is a setting the user has to re-apply by hand from a file that tells them so.
Silently losing one is not on the list.

## How Rime's own lines are told apart from the user's

Not by diffing against the shipped template. A config seeded by an OLDER image
does not line-match the current one — `$browser` used to be `firefox`, and the
window rules were spelled `suppressevent` / `nofocus` before 0.54 — so a text
diff would call almost every line a user edit and then duplicate the whole Rime
default set on top of the new modules.

Instead every Rime default this project has shipped is listed below and matched
SEMANTICALLY: a bind by its combo and its action, a setting by its section, key
and value. A bind whose combo is a Rime default but whose action differs is a
deliberate user rebind, and it is emitted as a disable() of the default plus the
user's own bind — never as a second bind on the same combo, which would
double-fire.

## Idempotence

By construction, not by a flag: the legacy files are RENAMED to timestamped
backups rather than copied, and the script refuses to run at all when
hyprland.lua already exists. A second run therefore finds nothing to migrate.
That also means a hyprland.lua the user has since edited is never rewritten.

usage:
  rime-hypr-migrate [--home DIR] [--dry-run] [--force] [--self-test]
"""
import argparse
import datetime
import os
import re
import shutil
import subprocess
import sys
import tempfile

BANNER = "Converted from hyprlang by rime-hypr-migrate"

# ── what Rime has ever shipped ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# Matched semantically, so an old seeded config is recognised as Rime's work
# even though it does not line-match the current template.

